text.body:
Two Lean statements cover the correction of P009-E004. The main statement holds for any design. If $i
e j$ and unit $i$ does not interfere with unit $j$, then the outcome of unit $j$ is the same for $z_i=1$ and $z_i=0$, whatever its own treatment and the other assignments, and $\xi_{ji}(0)=\xi_{ji}(1)= reve\xi_{ji}=0.$ Equivalently, $ reve\xi_{ji}
e0$ implies that unit $i$ interferes with unit $j$. This is the direction that the corrected sentences keep. The converse is the printed claim, and the example against it has a Lean statement of its own. The second statement is the last sentence of the printed passage: if there is no interference, then $\xi_{ij}(1)=\xi_{ij}(0)=0$ for all distinct $i$ and $j$, and $B_1=0$. It supposes in addition that $n\ge1$ and that $0<\Pr(Z_i=1)<1$ for every unit, so that the conditional expectations in $B_1$ are defined. In the paper this condition follows from Assumption 1A. The corrected wording that direct interference can give $ reve\xi_{ji}
e0$ is a remark on what is possible and is not formalized.
